Lots more.

That's what I suspected, you didn't provide the
mechanism to support the usual "first establish
credit" way to protect from the bad affects of
teleportitus, without also making a free cash gift to
the shopkeeper if you decide to buy nothing.

The precise reason for the existing "drop your money
on the floor before picking up the item" strategy is
that players _do_ want to pick up items they may not
at that moment have decided to buy.

Consider this very common scenario:

I go into an armor shop with my pet cat Clueless.

As Clueless steps on each interesting bit of armor,
I pick up that armor, try it on to check for
enchantments, name the piece with a name indicating
its enchantment level, take it off, and put it down.

[Probably in the case of something I might want to
buy, I put it in a special pile where I've dropped,
but not sold, some item I know to be cursed, to keep
Clueless from moving it away.]

That gets hugely dangerous with teleportitus, so, in
the existing implementation, I drop my cash, and
make sure I don't pick up anything more expensive
than my resulting credit level.

At the end, if I find nothing I want, I pick up my
cash and leave the shop, not out a zorkmid for my
precautions.

If I teleport out by teleportitus, I may buy
something I don't want, but at least I don't have a
shopkeeper and his minions after me, and if I
dropped more cash than the cost of the particular
item, I can safely go back to the shop and pick up
the difference in gold from the pile on the shop
floor; the shopkeeper is still peaceful.

In your version, even if I only pre-pay the
shopkeeper enough to cover the most expensive item I
want to wear to check its enchantment, if I find
nothing I want to buy, when I leave the shop I have
just made the shopkeeper a huge cash gift, because
there is no way to cash in my credit.

If I teleport out by accident, I cannot recover any
excess credit as cash by returning to the shop,
either.

Those are not desirable outcomes, and even people
who dislike credit cloning would be injured in the
wallet by your implementation of an alternative.

Armor isn't the only case where all this is an
issue. I might be willing to pay for using one
charge on something, like in identifying a wand by
engraving with it, where I wouldn't want to pay for
a whole wand if it turned out to be something
useless.  This is again a dangerous thing to do with
teleportitus, so you drop cash first to establish
credit in case you teleport out of the shop in debt,
but you drop excess, because you don't (yet) know
what that single zap will cost, and when you are
done testing wands, you want to be able to get back
the balance of your credit as cash before you leave
the shop. Now you can, with your patch you can't.

I hope that clarifies the issues.

